            <div xml:id="d923">

                <pb facs="0866" n="766" sameAs="frus:frus1920v03/pg766"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">867.77/317: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Commissioner at Berlin</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic">Dresel</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Berlin</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1920-06-04T20:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 4, 1920—8
                                p.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received June 6—8:09 p.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>562. From Coffin:<anchor n="10" xml:id="d923-f1"/></p>
                <p>“Von Gwinner of Deutsche Bank stated today that he can obtain control of
                    Anatolian Railway for American interests for $6,000,000. The control would carry
                    with it control of the Bagdad railway, Mersina-Adana line, port concessions at
                    Haidar Pasha and Alexan-dretta and petroleum rights owned by Anatolia and Bagdad
                    Railways. Anatolia Railway is now controlled by Bank für Orientalische
                    [Eisen]bahnen, a Swiss corporation. The project is very drawn [<hi
                        rendition="#italic">sic</hi>] owing to the obvious political complications
                    but it might develop very interesting features if the Turkish peace treaty
                    protects neutral interests. Can you ascertain and cable me whether any American
                    group would be sufficiently interested to investigate proposal fully in which
                    case I will hasten forwarding of full details.”</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Dresel</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d923-f1" n="10" xml:id="d923-f1-content">
                <p>William Coffin, U.S. consul general at Berlin.</p>
            </note>
